# open on the pin while `%308` -- folded onto a shelf the same day -- carried the only record of its
# closure. The split is structural: every fold moves a closing row into a closed stack and leaves
# the row it closed on the open shelf. Gates contradicted_rows and phantom_closures at zero, and
# reports open_rows, closed_rows, closure_claims, duplicate_row_lines and fold_blocked_rows. `lap`
# rather than `cadence`: it is awk over 128 markdown files and costs under a second, measured
# 20260828, and the page it reads is the page every lap chooses its work from.

guard reds_status_consistency
path tools/r/reds_status_consistency_witness.rish
seated 20260828.125441

# pin_bound_touch -- the seven living pins carry byte bounds so an agent reads one in a single
# breath beside its lap, and until 20260828 nothing weighed a pin at the moment a round moved it.
# REDS %293 named the mechanical repair in its own third field and left it unbuilt; the next day
# construction/REDS.md stood 3,992 bytes over and construction/ITINERARY.md 3 over, and four
# commits shipped across a red `equinox_e123_living_pin_guard`. Two readings that cannot disagree:
# e123 weighs every pin at roster time, this weighs only the ones the round's own diff names, off
# the index, where `git cat-file -s :<path>` is the size the commit will actually carry. One roster
# file and one bound reading (living_pin_max_bytes.sh) are shared, so there is no second roof.
# It also weighs EVERY touched pin before refusing -- e123 exits at its first, which is how the
# ledger's 3,992 bytes went unreported while the card's 3 were named. Armed as RULE THREE in
# tools/hooks/pre-commit, proven by 36 cases in a pen, five of them running real `git commit`s
# through the real hook. The witness gates only its own reading -- the refusals, the pen, the hook
# turning a real commit away -- and PRINTS the live worktree reading without asserting on it, so
# one fault reds in one place and e123 keeps that gate. `lap` rather than `cadence`: the witness
# costs 3s on this bench, measured 20260828, and the fault it refuses can be written by any lap
# that edits a pin.

guard pin_bound_touch
path tools/p/pin_bound_touch_witness.rish
seated 20260828.153046

# pond_enclosure_policy -- orbit one of the quest that retires ai-jail. pond/enclosure_policy.rye
# reads an enclosure as a value and marks each declaration place, hold, or refuse at receipt,
# naming the declaration that refused. The scan plants one forbidden mount and then removes it, so
# the refusal is proven from both sides; a refusal shown only in the passing direction cannot be
# told from a bypass. `lap` rather than `cadence`: the arc is under active work and the whole run
# costs one second warm, measured 20260828. Rostered here on the lap that recovered it, since an
# unrostered witness raises `unreached` and proves nothing on any tree but its author's.

guard pond_enclosure_policy
path tools/p/pond_enclosure_policy_witness.rish
tier lap
gate %5
seated 20260828.002135

# pond_enclosure_state -- the launcher's state house and the per-host config it reads, compared.
# tools/ag/agent-jail.sh seats `loops/claude`, `loops/cursor`, and `loops/codex` and adopts an
# elder directory into each once; tools/e/enclosure.conf is sourced BEFORE those defaults, so a
# path named there outranks the move and the jail binds a directory the adoption just emptied. The
# gate is one reading -- the tracked example naming an elder -- and the host's own gitignored conf
# and the directories on disk are reported without gating, since one machine's file is not the
# tree's to fail on. `lap` rather than `cadence`: the enclosure arc is under active work and the
# whole run costs under two seconds, measured 20260828. Booked as REDS %327 on the lap that found
# a credential directory already rebound empty on this pier.

guard pond_enclosure_state
path tools/p/pond_enclosure_state_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260828.170003

# pond_policy_launcher -- the enclosure record and the launcher it claims to describe, compared.
# pond/enclosure_policy.kyri declares what the agent-jail lap builds and tools/ag/agent-jail.sh
# builds it; two spellings of one contract stood side by side and nothing compared them, so the
# record carried `network off` for an enclosure sharing the host's own network namespace (REDS
