# tlb_reach -- a table is bounded twice, and only one bound appears in `@sizeOf`. The elder
# footprint study measured cost against bytes touched; a load's address is translated first, and
# that structure's capacity is counted in PAGES. Measured on this pier 20260906: 64 pages of span
# cost 1.65 ns per dependent read and 65 pages cost 3.90 -- same bytes, same instructions, same
# L1d sets, one more page -- and 2 MiB pages return the ratio to 1.00, which is how the cause is
# known. This rung proves the CENSUS rather than the reading: 38 planted behaviors, eight legs
# from the failing side, the clean baseline asserted before and after every plant, and each plant
# checked to have changed the reading it touched. The eighth leg, `elder_arm_ran`, gates the arm
# that reads the elder study's own three sizes under both page sizes -- gated for PRESENCE, since
# an arm that quietly did not run prints no attribution and reads exactly like one that ran and
# found nothing. The census itself stays UNROSTERED on purpose --
# 64 pages is a property of the board, so a guard asserting it would red the day the fleet moves
# part, and a guard that reds on ordinary work is one somebody turns off. The build leg is here
# because %449 and %463 measured what an uncompiled authored `.rye` costs. `tier lap`: 22s, of
# which 19 is that build.

guard tlb_reach
path tools/t/tlb_reach_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260906.061229
# amphora, the crossing -- the three modules the nine guards above never compile.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED (`20260906.063124`), and how. Every one of the 177 rostered paths standing that
# hour was read together with its own
# fixtures two levels deep (`awk '/^path /{print $2}'` over this file, then each guard's
# `tools/fixtures/...` references, then theirs). The eight amphora witnesses build exactly three
# modules -- `amphora/src/main.rye`, `vessel_core.rye`, `vessel_seal.rye` -- whose `@import`
# closure adds `manifest_entry.rye` and the two symlinked bounds files. `purchase_delivery.rye`,
# `vessel_fetch_wire.rye` and `vessel_fetch_delivery.rye` were compiled by NOTHING a lap runs:
# 1,160 lines, 32% of the 3,603 this lane owns.
#
# THE BLOCK ABOVE NAMED THIS GAP IN ITS OWN WORDS AND DID NOT CLOSE IT. It reads *a lap that
# broke a seal, a stamp, or the chunk assembler would have shipped green, because nothing was
# listening*, offering that as the state its eight rows cured -- and the chunk assembler is
# exactly these two wire modules, which `grep -l chunk amphora/*.rye` answers and no rostered
# guard compiled. Three comlink guards READ `vessel_fetch_wire.rye` as text for a bound tie
# (`tools/fixtures/c/comlink_carriage_scan.sh:68` binds it to `vessel=`) and none of the three
# builds it. That is REDS %449's shape one room over -- grep-reach standing in for compile-reach
# -- and in `mantra/` it had already bitten. Here it had not: all three build today, measured
# before these rows were written, so this is a coverage repair rather than a repair of rot.
# Booked as REDS %477 (`20260906.063124`). That row read `%466` while it sat unsent in a stash and
# `%472` while it sat in an unpushed commit; `xy` spent both in the meantime, and an unshared row
# renumbers -- the derived spine's own law, twice in one lap.
#
# `rye_compile_reach` READS ALL THREE AS `built`, and is right to. It asks whether ANY runner in
# the tree compiles a module; this roster asks whether a RUN one does. Its `built=1535` therefore
# counts runners nobody runs -- REDS %360's population seen from the other side -- so a green
# reading there settles the compile question and leaves the coverage question open. Two meters,
# two questions, and the second is the one that decides whether a lap would catch a break.
#
# THE HEADLINE ABOVE IS CORRECTED IN PLACE. *10 tracked Rye modules and 3,861 lines* counts three
# paths this lane does not own: `kumara.rye` and `tally_copy.rye` are symlinks into `tally/`, and
# `wire_format.rye` into `comlink/`. Seven modules and 3,603 lines is the lane's own figure. REDS
# %463's *resolution before accusation*, arriving in a roster comment rather than in a census.
#
# THE OTHER FIVE RUNNERS ARE MEASURED AND DECLINED, each by name rather than by omission.
# `amphora_lap1`, `amphora_lap2`, `amphora_vessel_seal` and `amphora_vessel_stamp` build only
# modules the nine above already compile, so they would add claims rather than reach, and the
# family already proves pour, carry, restore and three tamper refusals. `amphora_device_wire`
# drives `comlink/run_vessel_fetch_wire_lab.sh` under virtio and this pier holds no qemu. It
# REFUSES HONESTLY -- exit 1, and the one GREEN in its output is the substring an assert was
# looking for rather than a claim it made -- which is the good half of REDS %460, whose open
# question is whether such a witness may read GREEN with a named gap. Rostering it here would put
# an environment fact on a lap clock.
#
# `tier lap`, with the pier's state beside the number. GREEN at 3s, 3s and 2s -- 8 seconds
# together -- at load average 8.3 with the emitted binaries deleted first, so each rebuilt from
# source against a warm Zig cache (`20260906.063124`), against a full lap pass of 704 seconds
# measured the same hour. Re-measured warm at 2s, 2s and 1s three times each (`20260906.093709`)
# against a pass of 840. All twelve amphora rows together now cost roughly 16 of those 840.
#
# AND THE RUNNING FOUND WHAT THE READING COULD NOT. These three were rostered on a measurement of
# COVERAGE -- which modules a lap compiles -- and the first lap to actually run them repeatedly
# found `amphora/vessel_fetch_delivery.rye` stalling forever, one run in six to eight, alone on a
# quiet pier. Both halves of the module's socket habit were races: it opened, bound, received and
# closed a socket per datagram, so the receiver was unbound in every gap, and a UDP datagram
# arriving at an unbound port is discarded with no trace on either side; and `recvfrom` carried no
# `SO_RCVTIMEO`, so the receive that followed waited forever. The elder form did red -- at the
# selftest, naming a selftest that was fine -- because a failed `rye build` overwrites the emitted
# binary with a 5,547-byte artifact, which the warm-binary fallback beside it could never have
# survived. Measured, elder against repaired, same pier and same hour at load 8.5: 2 stalls in 20
# against 0 in 40. REDS %478 (`20260906.093709`).
#
# THREE THINGS STAND BEHIND THESE ROWS NOW, and they are different instruments. The module binds
# before it sends and holds one socket across an exchange, with every receive bounded and named --
# that is the repair. `tools/fixtures/a/amphora_vessel_port_lock.sh` holds UDP 38494/38495 at a
# HOST path around every step that speaks them, because a port belongs to the machine and eight
# trees reach for one pair -- that is what keeps two lawful runs from meeting. And the fixtures
# bound their two long-lived processes with `timeout`, so whatever slips past both reds in a minute
# rather than stopping the pass. Proven from both sides: two concurrent `chunkdemo` runs abort at
# 134 in 31 seconds unlocked, and pass in 1 to 2 seconds through the lock, three trials each.
#
# THE REFUSAL REACHES THE READER THROUGH THE SHIM. `tools/am/amphora_*.rish` are accrete shims onto
# `tools/gen/amphora/`, and they forwarded `r.out` alone -- so a planted break made the direct run
# say *no longer compiles* and the roster's own path say nothing at all, filing an evidence page
# naming the guard and no reason. The exit code always travelled; the sentence did not. These three
# forward `r.err` now, and the other five took the same two lines on `20260906.124500` -- all nine
# amphora shims carry their target's reason, and `shim_reason` below holds the habit for the tree.

