# shim_reason -- a pass-through shim hands on its target's reason, not only its verdict.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG, measured on a real file with no plant. `tools/am/*.rish` and their kin are accrete
# shims: each runs a target under `tools/gen/` and exits that target's code, so either path may be
# named and the answer is the same. Rishi's `run` captures the target's stderr into `r.err`, and a
# shim that says only `r.out` writes nothing to its own stderr. `amphora_device_wire` refuses
# honestly on this pier -- it drives a virtio lab and there is no qemu here -- so the two readings
# stand side by side without breaking anything: the target run directly says `rishi: assertion
# failed -- Amphora vessel fetch device wire lab failed`, with the line number; the shim run the same
# second exits 1 with ZERO bytes on stderr, and all a reader receives is `amphora-device-wire:
# virtio fetch-by-digest lab...`, a progress line printed BEFORE the failure, which reads like
# success. This runner captures a guard with `> out 2>&1`, so the merge was already there and caught
# nothing: the shim emitted nothing to merge.
#
# A GATE AND A RATCHET, because this is a trap rather than a live fault. Zero swallowing shims stand
# on this roster, so nothing is lost today; it springs on the lap that ROSTERS one, and %360's
# standing pressure is to roster more. The amphora lane walked into it on `20260906.063124` -- three
# shims onto a lap clock, the swallow found by planting a break and repaired in the same breath. The
# gate is what spares the next lane that discovery. The ratchet is the residue off the roster, and it
# only falls; the scan's own `unrostered_ceiling` line carries the number so no page can drift from
# it. Nine of 52 shims forward the reason today, and five of those nine were repaired in this lap.
#
# THE GATE IS PROVEN ABLE TO BITE, which matters more than usual here: `swallow_rostered` reads zero
# on this field and always has, so nothing in the tree could show it working. The pen supplies the
# case -- one swallowing shim named by a `path` row, refused; the same shim with one line added,
# freed. The ceiling is proven from both sides on ONE plant, two unrostered swallowers read at a
# ceiling of two and then of one.
#
# THREE MARKS, TOGETHER. A shim is the `run ["rishi/bin/rishi" "run" ...]` call, the `say r.out`
# forward, and the `exit r.code` tail, all three -- each alone is ordinary, since witnesses run
# witnesses and plenty of scripts say a captured stdout. A seat is a `path` row at column zero,
# never a path mentioned in a roster comment; unanchored, a guard could count as rostered because
# somebody wrote about it, and the pen plants that sentence. Two tracked files exit a captured run's
# code under another variable name and are invisible to the reading; neither is a shim, and both are
# named every pass as `exit_alias_sites`, so the floor carries a number (%466).
#
# `tier lap`, and cheap because the corpus arrives through one `git grep` whose status is classified
# rather than swallowed (%473, %484). Three greps over 2,362 tracked `.rish` files cost 22 seconds
# measured on this pier at load 10.1; the pre-filter reads the identical counts in 2, and the whole
# guard including its 40-case pen runs in 5 against a full lap pass of 729.

guard shim_reason
path tools/s/shim_reason_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260906.124500
# rye_struct_fields -- the reader four guards needed and none of them had (REDS %500). The four
# Mantra Glow Tend limbs each carry a placard declaring that a structure "carries exactly N
# fields", and each proved it with N `grep -Fq` calls for N field lines. Presence is not a count.
# A fourth field passed all four; a reorder passed all four; and all four refused in the words
# "missing or reordered". Two named movements of the Mantra arc -- the identity gap, wanting a
# fourth field on `Line`, and the diff anchor, wanting one on `Diff` -- were recorded on the
# operator card and in a commit body as waiting on that pin, which had no bolt in it. This rung
# proves the reader that replaced it: a count and an order out of the Rye source, three refusals
# by name, and TWO phases that run the elder predicate verbatim on the same mutated pen and watch
# it wave a fourth field and a reorder through. Those two passes are the finding itself. 19
# behaviors; `tier lap` at 1s, because the four consumers it feeds all run every lap.

guard rye_struct_fields
path tools/r/rye_struct_fields_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260906.140000
# The diameter floor, seated 20260906.143000. `comlink/topology.rye` publishes hop counts, and
# `topology_graph_census.sh` now reports the smallest diameter any graph of that many points and
# that maximum degree could hold -- the Moore bound solved for the DIAMETER rather than for the
# node count, which is the direction a designer's constraint actually binds. The reading exists
# because a paper asked the same bound the other way, answered nine billion nodes against our 720,
# and concluded a diameter argument decided nothing.
#
# WHY `cadence` RATHER THAN `lap`. The control runs the census seven times over 682,425 ordered
# pairs -- 151s measured, against 26s for one census pass. Its answer moves only when the census
# or the module moves, both deliberate acts, so a fifth-round reading is honest where a per-lap
# one would spend 151s a lap on eight ships watching a number that did not change.

guard topology_floor
path tools/t/topology_floor_witness.rish
tier cadence
seated 20260906.143000

# reds_citation -- a number used as a link is a promise about where the link goes (REDS row of
# `20260906.143706`). A row is cited two ways at once, as a number a reader recognises and as a path
# a reader clicks, and the two halves move at different moments: the number when the derived spine
# renumbers an unshared row, the path when a row folds onto a shelf. Nothing read them together.
# Four citations went out in one day naming one row and linking another, and each repair fixed the
# half the eye was on -- the number moved and the path stayed, then the path moved and the number
# stayed.
#
# EXISTENCE AND AGREEMENT ARE TWO QUESTIONS, and only one had an instrument. Measured on the tree
# that morning, `readme_reach` named ONE of the four -- the citation whose file was absent -- and
# read green over the other three, which open a real shelf holding somebody else's row. That is a
# misdirection with no expiry: it resolves, so it never reds, and a reader following it lands
# somewhere the sentence never meant. `tracked_link` asks existence the same way; `reds_row_present`
# asks whether the ledger HOLDS a row and never asks who cites it.
#
# TWO FORMS, AND THE THIRTY-TWO THAT TAUGHT THE NARROWING. A window around a link picks up every
# `%N` in the neighbouring prose, and a sentence may lawfully name one row while linking another --
# %220's own shelf cites %218's. A nearest-preceding-%N reading over 1,001 living documents called
# 32 honest sentences wrong, so the scan reads only the two shapes that are promises to a reader:
# the anchor text that IS the number, and the shelf word whose sentence just named a row. One line
# of lookback, never past the link, because this tree hard-wraps its pins and the break that shipped
# fell exactly across that wrap.
#
# PROVEN ON HISTORY AS WELL AS IN A PEN. Run against `b441f97b2`, this tree as it stood when the
# defect was published, the scan reads `numbered_disagree=3` and names all three sites. The pen adds
# 23 cases over 12 real repositories, every refusal planted and then lifted, and it caught a defect
# in the scan's own counting before it shipped -- `grep -c ''` prints 0 and exits 1 on an empty file,
# so the fallback beside it yielded two lines and the emptiness branch was skipped entirely.
#
# `tier lap`: one `git grep` over tracked markdown then awk over the two dozen documents that cite a
# shelf, about a second. Every fold moves a path and every renumbering moves a number, so ordinary
# work is what writes this fault, and a cadence clock would let one ride four laps.

guard reds_citation
path tools/r/reds_citation_witness.rish
tier lap
seated 20260906.143706
